  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 946 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions will plague the Mid-South through the\nend of the week as a 595 dam ridge meanders over the Southeast. A\nHeat Advisory will go into effect at 12PM on Tuesday for portions\nof the Mid-South. Additional heat headlines will be needed as we\nmove towards the end of the week. Maximum heat index values may\nreach 110 degrees in a few areas, but the probability of this\noccurring is about 20 percent. Should an Extreme Heat Warning be\nneeded, it will likely be for areas within the Mississippi Delta.\nOtherwise, expect air temperatures to be well above normal as\ndaily highs reach the upper 90s to low 100s.\n\nModels are beginning to pick up on a weak upper level disturbance\ndeveloping along the Gulf Coast by the end of the week. This\ndisturbance will bring a slight chance of precipitation Thursday\ninto the weekend, though rainfall totals will be negligible.\nInstead, the majority of the region will remain dry which will\nfurther exacerbate current drought conditions. The one silver\nlining to this forecast will be the arrival of a backdoor\ncold front on Sunday. Temperatures will not change much with this\nboundary, but lower dewpoints should negate the need for heat\nheadlines by Monday.\n\nBoth the GFS and ECMWF vary in regards to how the synoptic\npattern evolves next week. The GFS depicts a shortwave diving\nacross the Midwest, keeping the Mid-South within northwest flow\naloft. On the other hand, the ECMWF is hanging onto the\naforementioned ridge into the middle of next week. Regardless of\nwhich scenario manifests, there remains no sign of the Fall\nseason just yet.",
